---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { SHOWCASES, SITE } from '../lib/site';

const layout = `langium-tools/
langium-tools-showcases/
  statemachine/`;

const clone = `git clone https://github.com/borisbrodski/langium-tools.git
git clone https://github.com/borisbrodski/langium-tools-showcases.git
cd langium-tools && npm install && npm run build
cd ../langium-tools-showcases/statemachine
npm install
npm test`;

const diff = `git diff upstream -- statemachine`;

const markers = '<<{| … |}>>';
const servicesShape = '{ shared, statemachine }';

const javaImports = `const imports = new JavaImportManager('com.example');
const consumer = imports.useClass('java.util.function.Consumer');
// consumer === 'Consumer'
// imports.generateImports() === 'import java.util.function.Consumer;'`;
---
<Base
  title="Showcases — langium-tools"
  description="Working Langium languages that use langium-tools. The state machine keeps the official grammar and C++ program and adds issues, generation, Java imports, and snapshot tests."
>
  <article class="doc">
    <header class="doc-hero container">
      <div class="kicker">Examples</div>
      <h1>Showcases</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Each directory in <a href={SITE.showcases}>langium-tools-showcases</a> is one
        language. The projects do not share a package.json or a build. Add the next
        language as another directory and point it at the same sibling checkout of langium-tools.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-cta">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={SITE.showcases}>Repository</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="#statemachine">State machine</a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="container">
      <div class="grid3 showcase-grid">
        {SHOWCASES.map((item) => (
          <a class="card" href={`#${item.slug}`}>
            <h3>{item.title}</h3>
            <p>{item.blurb}</p>
            <span class="more">Read the walkthrough →</span>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>

      <h2>Clone it next to the library</h2>
      <p>
        The state machine depends on <span class="inline-code">file:../../langium-tools</span>.
        This package publishes <span class="inline-code">dist/</span>, so build the library
        before <span class="inline-code">npm install</span> in the showcase. Node must be
        <span class="inline-code">^22.22.2</span>, <span class="inline-code">^24.15.0</span>, or <span class="inline-code">&gt;=26</span>.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock code={layout} lang="text" filename="directories" />
      <CodeBlock code={clone} lang="bash" filename="terminal" />
      <p>
        <span class="inline-code">npm test</span> builds the language and compares
        <span class="inline-code">generated/</span> with <span class="inline-code">GENERATOR_TEST=verify</span>.
        <span class="inline-code">npm run test:generate</span> rewrites those snapshots.
      </p>

      <h2 id="statemachine">State machine</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={SHOWCASES[0].href}>statemachine</a> is the official Langium state-machine
        example. The grammar and the C++ program stay that example. langium-tools is the
        CLI report, the file collector, the Java event file, and the tests around them.
        Branch <span class="inline-code">upstream</span> is the official copy.
        <span class="inline-code">main</span> is the extension. The diff is the lesson.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock code={diff} lang="bash" filename="langium-tools-showcases" />

      <h3>Issues in the CLI</h3>
      <p>
        <a href="/issues/"><span class="inline-code">getDocumentIssueSummary</span></a> is the
        only error report. Lexer, parser, and validation errors share one count. Warnings
        are printed and generation continues. Errors stop the command.
      </p>

      <h3>Markers in the tests</h3>
      <p>
        <a href="/matchers/"><span class="inline-code">toHaveNoErrors</span></a> ignores warnings.
        <span class="inline-code">toHaveNoIssues</span> includes them. A lowercase state name is
        the sample that shows the difference, so a style warning does not fail a parse test.
        <span class="inline-code">parseWithMarks</span> and
        <span class="inline-code">toHaveDocumentIssues</span> use
        <span class="inline-code">{markers}</span>
        as the expected range. The markers are stripped before Langium sees the text.
      </p>

      <h3>Generate, then write</h3>
      <p>
        <span class="inline-code">generate</span> only calls
        <a href="/generator/"><span class="inline-code">createFile</span></a>.
        The CLI calls <span class="inline-code">writeToDisk</span>. Identical bytes keep
        their modification time. The same path with different text throws before a file
        is overwritten. The C++ templates themselves are unchanged. A Java file for the
        events is a second artifact of the same model. Pass workspace directories as
        <span class="inline-code">URI.file</span>. A raw filesystem string goes through
        <span class="inline-code">URI.parse</span> and is not a file URI.
      </p>

      <h3>Snapshot tests</h3>
      <p>
        <a href="/testing/"><span class="inline-code">langiumGeneratorSuite</span></a> builds
        each workspace under <span class="inline-code">test/generator/</span>, generates in
        memory, and compares content and the overwrite flag to
        <span class="inline-code">generated/</span> and
        <span class="inline-code">generated-metadata.json</span>.
        <span class="inline-code">createServices</span> returns the official
        <span class="inline-code">{servicesShape}</span> pair, which is the
        one non-shared service the suite requires.
      </p>

      <h3>Java events</h3>
      <p>
        <a href="/java/"><span class="inline-code">JavaImportManager</span></a> and
        <span class="inline-code">packageToPath</span> build
        <span class="inline-code">com/example/TrafficLightEvents.java</span>.
        <span class="inline-code">useClass</span> runs before
        <span class="inline-code">generateImports</span>. The current package is not imported.
        A second type with the same simple name stays fully qualified. The file is written
        with <span class="inline-code">createFile</span>. An empty event list cannot be a
        Java enum, so that model emits a final class with a private constructor.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock code={javaImports} filename="generator.ts" />
      <div class="note">
        <p>
          <span class="inline-code">generateJavaFile</span> is the one-call wrapper in the
          <a href="/java/">Java guide</a>. It builds the file with generator nodes from the
          Langium install inside langium-tools. A project that also depends on Langium has
          a second copy, and those nodes print as <span class="inline-code">[object Object]</span>.
          The showcase calls <span class="inline-code">JavaImportManager</span> and
          <span class="inline-code">createFile</span>. The note is in
          <span class="inline-code">statemachine/src/cli/generator.ts</span>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>Left for a language that needs them</h3>
      <p>
        <span class="inline-code">arraySortBy</span> would reorder events, and declaration
        order is the language order. <span class="inline-code">adjustedUnix</span> is for
        templates whose newlines must be <span class="inline-code">\n</span> on every
        platform. The snapshots here already are. <span class="inline-code">toFirstUpper</span>
        and <span class="inline-code">toFirstLower</span> are unused because the names
        already fit the output. The <span class="inline-code">.cpp</span> and
        <span class="inline-code">.java</span> files share one output directory, so there
        is no named generator target. A named target is the next step when those files
        must land in different directories. Once any custom target is passed to the suite,
        every target, including the default, is written under
        <span class="inline-code">generated/&lt;name&gt;/</span>.
      </p>

      <div class="next-guides">
        <a href={SHOWCASES[0].href}>State machine on GitHub →</a>
        <a href="/issues/">Document issues</a>
        <a href="/generator/">Code generation</a>
        <a href="/testing/">Generator tests</a>
        <a href="/matchers/">Vitest matchers</a>
        <a href="/java/">Java</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
  <style>
    .hero-cta { display: flex; gap: 14px; margin: 8px 0 8px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
    .showcase-grid { margin: 28px 0 8px; }
  </style>
</Base>
